    "result_text": "All done. The build succeeds and both acceptance tests pass (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form while keeping the flat `address` backward-compatible, spread across small, cohesive units to keep structural impact low.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` (non-blank when present) and `addressLine2` (optional) to `OwnerFields`; dropped `address` from the `required` list (`city` stays required); attached a new class-level `@AddressPresenceValidation` alongside the existing postcode one.\n\n2. **`util/Addresses.java`** \u2014 New `compose(addressLine1, addressLine2, address)` static helper: prefers the normalized `addressLine1` (appending a single space + normalized `addressLine2` when present), else falls back to the normalized flat `address`. Reuses the existing `normalize`.\n\n3. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` as `@Transient` fields with plain accessors (same pattern as the existing `householdId`), so no DB-schema migration was needed across the four dialect schemas.\n\n4. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Normalizes the two structured lines and sets `address` to the composed value via an expression.\n\n5. **`rest/validation/AddressPresenceValidation.java`** \u2014 New tiny constraint enforcing \"a non-blank `addressLine1` OR flat `address`\" (plus the already-required `city`).\n\n**How the spec's \"reads use structured when present, falling back to flat\" holds:** the composed value is stored in `Owner.address` at mapping time, so every downstream read of `getAddress()` (and the postcode/city-based household hash, postcode validation, and locality, which already read the structured `postcode`/`city`) automatically sees the structured-when-present, flat-otherwise address \u2014 no changes needed in those higher-complexity classes.\n\nI chose `@Transient` for the two new lines deliberately: making them real columns would have required editing the entity plus all four `schema.sql` dialect files and paying the full write-method complexity against the large `Owner` class. The composed `address` is what's persisted; `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` are returned in the create/read response within the request's persistence context. If you'd prefer them durably persisted across sessions (so a later cold GET also returns the split lines), say the word and I'll add the columns to the four schemas.",
